<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Turn Off the Lights, Please.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories and news items. Interactive: Contribute actively to group projects and discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend the main idea from short announcements about conserving energy (e.g., "Turn off the lights and conserve energy."). Can actively follow and contribute to a group project on community energy conservation (e.g., "We can reduce energy consumption."). Can follow oral instructions to save energy (e.g., "Use natural light.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and vocabulary related to energy conservation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays pictures of a bright sunny day, a room with lights on, a dark room, and a dripping faucet. The teacher asks: "What do you see?" and elicits "sun," "lights," "water." The teacher then plays a short audio clip of a faucet dripping and asks: "What do you hear?" "Is this good or bad?" The teacher introduces the theme: "Today we will learn about saving energy!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a light switch and says, "Turn off the light!" while miming the action. The teacher points to a window and says, "Use natural light." The teacher introduces core vocabulary by saying the words clearly and showing flashcards/actions: "light," "shade," "water," "energy," "turn off," "save," "conserve." The teacher models the pronunciation of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off" by over-articulating the sounds and having students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When it's sunny, do we need to turn on the lights?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it good to save water?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we do with the light when we leave a room?" (Expected answer: "Turn off the light.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio announcements (e.g., public service announcements for kids) and simple illustrated sentences. The content focuses on daily energy-saving actions using imperatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehend the gist of short announcements about conserving energy and follow oral instructions to save energy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays short audio clips (20-30 seconds each) of announcements related to energy conservation, e.g., "Good morning! Turn off the lights when you leave the room. Save energy!" or "At night, remember to switch off all electricity. It's important for the environment."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the announcements. The teacher then displays corresponding large, simple pictures or icons for each announcement (e.g., a hand turning off a light, a closed faucet).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each announcement, the teacher asks: "What is the announcement about?" (Expected: "Lights," "Water," "Energy.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays all announcements again. Students listen and point to the correct picture for each announcement.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher makes statements based on the audio. "We should waste water." (False) "We should recycle." (True). Students say "True" or "False" or show T/F cards.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, preparing students to use the language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening for imperatives and target vocabulary: "turn off," "save," "conserve," "reduce," "waste," "electricity," "bulbs," "faucet," "environment." Practicing /sh/ and /t/ sounds and distinguishing "-re" (recycle) and "-er" (safer).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with sentences containing gaps (cloze activity). The teacher replays the audio announcements from Stage 2. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing imperative verbs or nouns.
                            <br>Example: "____ off the lights." (Turn) "Close the ____." (faucet) "Let's ____ energy." (save) "Recycle the ____." (bulbs).
                            <br>The teacher emphasizes the sounds: "/sh/ade," "/t/urn off," "/r/ecycle," "saf/er/."
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher reads short sentences or phrases. Students listen and choose the correct option from two or three choices.
                            <br>Example: Teacher says: "It's bright outside." Students choose: a) Turn on the lights. b) Use natural light. (Listening for contextual clues and imperatives).
                            <br>Teacher says: "Don't waste water." Students choose: a) Open the faucet. b) Close the faucet.
                            <br>The teacher introduces comparatives: "Saving energy is *easier* than wasting it." "Recycling is *safer* for the environment."
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check: Listen and Repeat:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays new short audio instructions about conservation. One student listens and repeats the instruction, then the other student draws a simple picture to represent it. Then they switch roles.
                            <br>Example audio: "Switch off the TV at night." "Put old bulbs in the recycle bin." "Use *cooler* lights for the morning." "This is the *most* important thing to do."
                            <br>They check their partner's drawing and comprehension. The teacher monitors for accurate listening and pronunciation of target vocabulary and sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative listening and instruction-giving activity (Group Project Brainstorm).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Our Eco-Friendly Home Plan"</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher distributes a large blank paper titled "Our Eco-Friendly Home Plan" and some markers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays an audio scenario describing a "typical family day" with some energy waste (e.g., "The family leaves the lights on during the day. Water runs while brushing teeth. The TV is on but no one is watching."). The audio will be slightly longer than previous clips, with a clear narrative.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the scenario. After listening, they discuss in their groups what actions the family should take to save energy. They must use imperatives and the target vocabulary.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they discuss, they draw or write simple sentences on their "Eco-Friendly Home Plan" poster, illustrating their solutions.
                            <br>Example dialogue:
                            <br>Student A (after listening): "The TV is on in the morning and no one is watching!"
                            <br>Student B: "Yes! So, *turn off the TV*."
                            <br>Student C: "And the faucet is open. *Close the faucet* to *save water*."
                            <br>Student D: "Using natural light is *easier* in the morning. *Open the curtains*."
                            <br>Student A: "We should *recycle* old *bulbs*. It's *the most important* way to help the *environment*."
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension and guiding groups to use imperatives, comparatives, and the target vocabulary (morning, night, electricity, reduce, help, environment).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Our Eco-Friendly Home Plan" activity, focusing on listening comprehension and participation, plus a quick exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students collectively present one or two solutions from their "Eco-Friendly Home Plan" poster to the class, orally stating the problem they heard and their recommended action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can comprehend main idea from audio scenario.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively follows and contributes to group project (listening to peers, offering solutions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows oral instructions to save energy (e.g., uses natural light, turns off lights).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives correctly (e.g., "Turn off," "Close," "Save").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., light, water, energy, recycle, environment, bulbs, faucet).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Name one new word you learned about saving energy today.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Write one sentence telling me how you can save energy at home.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Personal reflection for both students and teacher.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was *easy* to listen to today? What was a little *more difficult*?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is *the most important* thing you learned about saving energy?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., visual support for listening, group collaboration).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., clearer audio, more repetition of new vocabulary, extra practice for pronunciation of /sh/ and /t/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to comprehend the main idea of the audio and follow instructions? Was the vocabulary and grammar level appropriate?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short text or story that uses the same vocabulary and imperatives to reinforce comprehension of energy conservation in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>